      en: "In the arid environment of the Diffa region, fruit gardens are precious pockets of fertility and abundance. These gardens are typically located along the Komadougou Yobé riverbed or in low-lying areas where water is accessible. Here, farmers cultivate a surprising variety of fruits, including dates from date palms, guavas, and citrus. Date palms are particularly important, as they are well-adapted to the climate and their fruit is a high-energy food source that can be stored for long periods. These gardens are managed intensively, often with hand-dug wells providing the necessary irrigation. They contribute to nutritional diversity and provide a valuable source of income in a region dominated by grain farming and pastoralism. These fruit gardens are a testament to the horticultural skill and resilience of the local people."
